            <ArticleTitle>Critical Elements of a School Report to Parents on Body Mass Index.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E136</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5888/pcd12.150165</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>School-based body mass index (BMI) screening and reporting could have a positive impact on student health, but best practices for writing a report are unknown. Building on previous qualitative work, 8 focus groups were conducted with a diverse group of California parents (n = 79) to elicit feedback on report content and design. Results indicate that parents want a visually appealing, picture-heavy report that clearly defines BMI, avoids stigmatizing language, and includes recommendations for appropriate actions whole families can take. Next steps involve using the final report in a statewide, randomized trial to determine the effectiveness of school-based BMI screening and reporting in reducing childhood obesity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
